{
 "case:accusation.methods.0": "Pushed her off the stepladder onto the iron staging",
 "case:accusation.methods.1": "Struck her down with the silver knob of her own stick",
 "case:accusation.methods.2": "Poisoned the cocoa Agnes took her",
 "case:accusation.methods.3": "Smothered her with her own shawl",
 "case:accusation.motives.0": "To keep her husband’s forgery from the police — and Penhallow in his hands",
 "case:accusation.motives.1": "To sell the moon orchid’s seed to an American nursery",
 "case:accusation.motives.2": "To save the gardens from being sold for a holiday camp",
 "case:accusation.motives.3": "To keep a secret engagement from coming out",
 "case:accusation.motives.4": "To be free after twenty years as a paid companion",
 "case:accusation.proofLabels.0": "Opportunity — with her when she died",
 "case:accusation.proofLabels.1": "The false widow — who rang the bell at 11.32",
 "case:accusation.proofLabels.2": "Motive — why it had to be Friday night",
 "case:accusation.successTitle": "Case closed",
 "case:briefing.0": "Penhallow House, Cornwall. Saturday 27 July 1963, ten o’clock in the morning.",
 "case:briefing.1": "For twenty years the village has called Lady Hester Penhallow the Glasshouse Widow. Since the telegram from Burma in 1943 she has worn black, veil and all, and lived among the plants her husband sent home — above all the moon orchid, which had never once flowered in England. Last night, for one night only, it bloomed.",
 "case:briefing.2": "She kept her vigil alone, locked in the glasshouse, and promised to ring the bell when it opened. At 11.32 the bell rang, and four people on the terrace saw her through the glass, lamp in hand beside the great white flower. At a quarter to six this morning the gardener found her dead at the foot of a stepladder.",
 "case:briefing.3": "On Tuesday she wrote asking you to come: someone in her family had done ‘something criminal’. Her letter reached you too late. Sergeant Polglase of St Austell has a theory and a suspect, and has given you the morning before the coroner’s officer arrives.",
 "case:briefing.4": "Find out who killed Lady Penhallow, how it was done and why — and be ready to prove it.",
 "case:epilogue.0": "Celia Penhallow held out for two days. Then Sergeant Polglase laid Dr. Ellery’s photograph in front of her: the tall black figure with the lamp held high. She looked at it for a long time, and then she said, quite calmly, ‘I always did play to the gallery.’",
 "case:epilogue.1": "Hester had told her at six o’clock: Julian had forged her name on a mortgage of Home Farm, and in the morning the deed would go to Mr. Kittow, and from Mr. Kittow to the police. At twenty to eleven Celia went down to the glasshouse to beg. Hester said that Julian would answer for it, as Edmund would have wished. Her stick was leaning against the bench.",
 "case:epilogue.2": "Afterwards Celia did what eleven years in repertory had taught her: she waited for her cue. She sat with the body for three-quarters of an hour while the moon orchid opened beside it. Then she put on the veil and the shawl, rang the bell and gave the performance of her life to an audience of four. Afterwards she dressed Hester in them again, locked the door so that no doctor would see the body before morning, burned the deed in the stokehole, and went in to drink champagne.",
 "case:epilogue.3": "She had studied everything about Hester Penhallow except the one thing that mattered. Hester would never have let that flower open and close untouched.",
 "case:epilogue.4": "Julian Penhallow was sent to prison for three years for forgery. The new will was never signed, so Penhallow came to him after all, and from his cell he gave it to the National Trust, gardens, glasshouse and all. Marian Ellery turned down Mr. Lindqvist’s twenty thousand dollars and took the seed to Kew. Edwin Kittow kept the ring he had bought in Truro for September.",
 "case:epilogue.5": "In November a single green pod ripened on the moon orchid. The first seedlings flowered at Kew in the spring of 1971. Jory Trevenna travelled up in his Sunday suit to see them, and chose their name: they are registered as ‘Glasshouse Widow’. On the last Friday of every July he still sits up all night in the glasshouse. Somebody ought to, he says.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.0": "Review your board. Who was with Hester when she died, who rang the bell at 11.32, and who needed her silenced before morning?",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.1": "Every part of the accusation needs a card: opportunity, the false widow and the motive. If one is missing, the hints above will have pointed you to it. Re-read your notebook.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse.levels.2": "The killer is Celia Penhallow: struck down with the silver knob of Hester’s own stick, to keep Julian’s forgery from the police. Prove it with Celia’s admission that she went to the glasshouse, her coke-dusted dressing gown and mules, and the forgery deduction.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse_ready.levels.0": "You may already have enough for an arrest. Who was with Hester when she died, who rang the bell, and why did it have to be Friday night?",
 "case:hints.h_accuse_ready.levels.1": "Only one person missed the bell, was in the glasshouse that night, and knew what Hester meant to do in the morning.",
 "case:hints.h_accuse_ready.levels.2": "Accuse Celia Penhallow: struck down with the silver knob of Hester’s own stick, to keep her husband’s forgery from the police. Prove it with her admission that she went to the glasshouse (or the timeline), her dressing gown and mules (or Jory’s or Dr. Ellery’s statement), and your forgery deduction (or the letter from Lorimer & Dane).",
 "case:hints.h_bell_ringer.levels.0": "Compare the woman at the bell with the woman in the 9.25 photograph.",
 "case:hints.h_bell_ringer.levels.1": "Look at her height against the lamp bracket, at her hands, and at her hem. Lady Penhallow couldn’t take a single step without something.",
 "case:hints.h_bell_ringer.levels.2": "Answer ‘Someone wearing her veil and shawl’, with the 11.32 photograph and the 9.25 photograph (or the ebony stick).",
 "case:hints.h_bloom.levels.0": "Hester had waited twenty-five years for that flower. Did she get her chance?",
 "case:hints.h_bloom.levels.1": "Read her bloom log, or ask Dr. Ellery what Hester would have done the moment it opened. Then remember what Jory found at dawn.",
 "case:hints.h_bloom.levels.2": "Answer ‘No — she would never have left it unpollinated’, with Jory’s dawn statement (or the moon orchid) and the bloom log (or Dr. Ellery’s statement about pollinating).",
 "case:hints.h_celia_alive.levels.0": "Celia swears Hester was alive and writing when she left at ten to eleven. Check what Hester wrote.",
 "case:hints.h_celia_alive.levels.1": "Hester logged the flower every quarter of an hour.",
 "case:hints.h_celia_alive.levels.2": "Challenge Celia’s ‘alive, writing in her log’ statement with the bloom log, or with your time-of-death deduction.",
 "case:hints.h_celia_knew.levels.0": "Celia says she knew nothing about any forgery. Who would have told her?",
 "case:hints.h_celia_knew.levels.1": "Read Hester’s note on the Lorimer & Dane letter again — or ask Mr. Kittow what Hester told him.",
 "case:hints.h_celia_knew.levels.2": "Challenge Celia’s ‘know nothing whatever about any forgery’ statement with the letter from Lorimer & Dane, or with Mr. Kittow’s statement about the engagement.",
 "case:hints.h_celia_rose.levels.0": "Celia says she walked in the rose walk and never went near the glasshouse. Does anything of hers say otherwise?",
 "case:hints.h_celia_rose.levels.1": "Whoever rang the bell wore something pale under the shawl and left by the stokehole. Look in the Blue Room wardrobe, or ask Dr. Ellery about the scent.",
 "case:hints.h_celia_rose.levels.2": "Challenge Celia’s ‘rose walk’ statement with her dressing gown and mules, the prints in the coke dust, or Jory’s or Dr. Ellery’s statement.",
 "case:hints.h_forgery.levels.0": "What was the ‘something criminal’ in her letter?",
 "case:hints.h_forgery.levels.1": "A signature tells you more once you know what ink she used. Ask Agnes about her habits, and search the boiler’s ash pan.",
 "case:hints.h_forgery.levels.2": "Answer ‘Proof that Julian forged her signature on a £20,000 mortgage’, with the letter from Lorimer & Dane and the charred deed (or Agnes’s statement about green ink).",
 "case:hints.h_jory_dawn.levels.0": "Someone was in the glasshouse at dawn, before the police. What did they see?",
 "case:hints.h_jory_dawn.levels.1": "Jory Trevenna found her.",
 "case:hints.h_jory_dawn.levels.2": "Interrogate Jory and ask him how he found her.",
 "case:hints.h_jory_pipe.levels.0": "Jory says he was in his bed by ten. Was he?",
 "case:hints.h_jory_pipe.levels.1": "Someone stood a long while by the walled-garden door last night, smoking.",
 "case:hints.h_jory_pipe.levels.2": "Examine the walled-garden door in the Terrace & Gardens, then challenge Jory’s ‘in my bed by ten’ statement with the pipe dottle.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_alibi.levels.0": "Now that you know when she died, look again at the sergeant’s suspect.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_alibi.levels.1": "Mrs. Pascoe’s statement says where Julian was from a quarter past ten until ten past eleven.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_alibi.levels.2": "Answer ‘No — he was in the library with Kittow while she died’, with Mrs. Pascoe’s statement (or Mr. Kittow’s) and your time-of-death deduction.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_forgery.levels.0": "Julian swears he kept his business afloat honestly.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_forgery.levels.1": "Lady Penhallow’s writing desk says otherwise.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_forgery.levels.2": "Challenge Julian’s ‘kept it afloat honestly’ statement with the letter from Lorimer & Dane.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_lawn.levels.0": "Julian says he never left the drawing room after the bell. The police think otherwise.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_lawn.levels.1": "Re-read Sergeant Polglase’s notes, or ask Agnes Quill what she saw from her window.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_lawn.levels.2": "Challenge Julian’s ‘never left the drawing room’ statement with the sergeant’s notes, or Agnes’s statement about the lawn.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_money.levels.0": "Julian says his affairs are in perfectly good order.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_money.levels.1": "His dinner jacket is thrown over a chair in the Blue Room.",
 "case:hints.h_julian_money.levels.2": "Challenge Julian’s ‘perfectly good order’ statement with the letter from the Westminster Bank.",
 "case:hints.h_kittow.levels.0": "Mr. Kittow says it was all entirely professional.",
 "case:hints.h_kittow.levels.1": "Lady Penhallow kept a letter in her blotter.",
 "case:hints.h_kittow.levels.2": "Challenge Mr. Kittow’s ‘entirely professional’ statement with his letter from her blotter.",
 "case:hints.h_marian.levels.0": "Dr. Ellery says she is here for Kew. Is she?",
 "case:hints.h_marian.levels.1": "Her satchel holds a cable from rather further away.",
 "case:hints.h_marian.levels.2": "Challenge Dr. Ellery’s ‘on behalf of Kew’ statement with the cable from Miami.",
 "case:hints.h_photo.levels.0": "Four people saw her at 11.32. Did any of them keep a record of what they saw?",
 "case:hints.h_photo.levels.1": "Dr. Ellery had her camera on the terrace when the bell rang.",
 "case:hints.h_photo.levels.2": "Ask Dr. Ellery what she saw when the bell rang, then look at the fresh print in her Darkroom.",
 "case:hints.h_scene.levels.0": "Crime scenes are generous to those who look twice. Have you been over the glasshouse properly?",
 "case:hints.h_scene.levels.1": "Look at what she is supposed to have fallen from, what she walked with, and what she wrote.",
 "case:hints.h_scene.levels.2": "In the Glasshouse, examine the stepladder, the ebony stick and the log table.",
 "case:hints.h_scent.levels.0": "The moon orchid’s scent clings to anyone who has been near it.",
 "case:hints.h_scent.levels.1": "Dr. Ellery knows that scent better than anyone. Who came into the drawing room after the bell?",
 "case:hints.h_scent.levels.2": "Ask Dr. Ellery whether she noticed anything when Celia came down at 11.45.",
 "case:hints.h_sitting.levels.0": "Lady Penhallow’s own papers may tell you what she meant to hand her solicitor.",
 "case:hints.h_sitting.levels.1": "Her sitting room is locked. Her companion keeps the key.",
 "case:hints.h_sitting.levels.2": "Ask Agnes Quill whether you may see Lady Penhallow’s rooms.",
 "case:hints.h_struck.levels.0": "Dr. Tresidder says she fell from the steps. Does the glasshouse agree with him?",
 "case:hints.h_struck.levels.1": "Compare the wound in the sergeant’s notes with her stick. Then ask yourself whether anyone climbed that ladder.",
 "case:hints.h_struck.levels.2": "On the board, answer ‘Struck down with the silver knob of her own stick’, with the ebony stick and the stepladder (or the iron staging).",
 "case:hints.h_timeline.levels.0": "Every event of the night is now on the record. Can you put them in order?",
 "case:hints.h_timeline.levels.1": "Open the timeline in your Notebook. Anchor the events whose times you know, then reason about the rest.",
 "case:hints.h_timeline.levels.2": "The true order: she locks herself in, Agnes’s cocoa, the last log entry, Celia goes down, the blow, the flower opens, the bell, the figure from the stokehole, Celia in the drawing room, Julian at the door, Jory at dawn.",
 "case:hints.h_tod.levels.0": "If she died before the flower opened, when did it open?",
 "case:hints.h_tod.levels.1": "Her last entry gives the time of the first scent — and how long Edmund said the flower takes to open.",
 "case:hints.h_tod.levels.2": "Answer ‘Between 10.25 and about 11.05 pm’, with the bloom log and your ‘Dead before the flower opened’ deduction.",
 "case:hints.h_way_out.levels.0": "The glasshouse door was locked from inside. So how did the bell-ringer get out?",
 "case:hints.h_way_out.levels.1": "Ask Jory about the stokehole door, and look again at the stokehole steps.",
 "case:hints.h_way_out.levels.2": "Answer ‘Down through the stokehole and out by its outer door’, with the stokehole door (or Jory’s statement about it) and the prints in the coke dust (or the charred deed).",
 "case:hints.h_who_absent.levels.0": "Four people watched from the terrace when the bell rang. Who didn’t?",
 "case:hints.h_who_absent.levels.1": "The sergeant’s notes say who missed the bell. Ask her where she was, then look out of her bedroom window.",
 "case:hints.h_who_absent.levels.2": "Ask Celia Penhallow where she was last night, examine the window in the Blue Room, and challenge her ‘watched Hester from my window’ statement with it.",
 "case:locations.blue_room.art.props.0.label": "Window — north",
 "case:locations.blue_room.art.props.1.label": "Dressing table",
 "case:locations.blue_room.art.props.2.label": "Bed",
 "case:locations.blue_room.art.props.3.label": "Wardrobe",
 "case:locations.blue_room.art.props.4.label": "Armchair",
 "case:locations.blue_room.art.props.5.label": "Rug",
 "case:locations.blue_room.description": "The best guest bedroom on the north front, papered in faded blue. The bed has been slept in on both sides. Julian’s clothes are thrown over a chair; Celia’s are put away.",
 "case:locations.blue_room.hotspots.hs_br_bed.label": "Bed",
 "case:locations.blue_room.hotspots.hs_br_bed.text": "Slept in on both sides. On Julian’s side, a bottle of sleeping tablets: he took two when he came up at one, he told the sergeant, and slept like the dead until the alarm. Whatever his wife did in the night, he wouldn’t have known.",
 "case:locations.blue_room.hotspots.hs_br_chair.label": "Julian’s jacket",
 "case:locations.blue_room.hotspots.hs_br_chair.text": "Julian’s dinner jacket is thrown over the armchair. Something crackles in the inside pocket.",
 "case:locations.blue_room.hotspots.hs_br_dressing.label": "Dressing table",
 "case:locations.blue_room.hotspots.hs_br_dressing.text": "Among the scent bottles, a silver-framed photograph of a young actress in a crown and a long gown: ‘Celia Lane as Lady Macbeth, Bristol Old Vic, 1954’. Even in a photograph, she holds the eye.",
 "case:locations.blue_room.hotspots.hs_br_wardrobe.label": "Wardrobe",
 "case:locations.blue_room.hotspots.hs_br_wardrobe.text": "The figure at the bell showed a pale hem under the black shawl, and left by the stokehole. You open the wardrobe.",
 "case:locations.blue_room.hotspots.hs_br_window.label": "Window",
 "case:locations.blue_room.hotspots.hs_br_window.text": "You draw back the curtain and look out.",
 "case:locations.blue_room.name": "The Blue Room",
 "case:locations.blue_room.summary": "Julian and Celia’s bedroom.",
 "case:locations.darkroom.art.props.0.label": "Blacked-out window",
 "case:locations.darkroom.art.props.1.label": "Stone sink",
 "case:locations.darkroom.art.props.2.label": "Bench",
 "case:locations.darkroom.art.props.3.label": "Safelight",
 "case:locations.darkroom.art.props.4.label": "Enlarger",
 "case:locations.darkroom.art.props.5.label": "Drying line",
 "case:locations.darkroom.art.props.6.label": "Dresser",
 "case:locations.darkroom.art.props.7.label": "To the passage",
 "case:locations.darkroom.description": "The old scullery off the back passage, which Dr. Ellery has blacked out and filled with chemicals. It smells of vinegar and fixer. The red safelight still glows over the sink.",
 "case:locations.darkroom.hotspots.hs_dr_line.label": "Prints on the line",
 "case:locations.darkroom.hotspots.hs_dr_line.text": "Prints hang from the drying line on wooden pegs: the orchid’s bud, the glasshouse by daylight — and one of Lady Penhallow herself.",
 "case:locations.darkroom.hotspots.hs_dr_newprint.label": "A fresh print",
 "case:locations.darkroom.hotspots.hs_dr_newprint.text": "At the end of the line, still damp and curling: the print Dr. Ellery made this morning from her one exposure at the bell.",
 "case:locations.darkroom.hotspots.hs_dr_safelight.label": "Safelight",
 "case:locations.darkroom.hotspots.hs_dr_safelight.text": "A red lamp over the sink. When it’s lit the door must stay shut, or a night’s work is ruined. A perfect place to be alone, and nobody can see that you are there.",
 "case:locations.darkroom.hotspots.hs_dr_satchel.label": "Dr. Ellery’s satchel",
 "case:locations.darkroom.hotspots.hs_dr_satchel.text": "A canvas satchel hangs from a hook by the dresser: notebooks, light meter, spare film. And a cable form, folded very small.",
 "case:locations.darkroom.hotspots.hs_dr_trays.label": "Developing trays",
 "case:locations.darkroom.hotspots.hs_dr_trays.text": "Three enamel trays and a notebook in a neat hand: ‘10.05 — bud negatives in the tank. 10.40 — first prints. 10.50 — Mrs. P. at the door!! 11.10 — washed; out to the terrace.’ The prints from those negatives hang on the line, dated and numbered.",
 "case:locations.darkroom.name": "The Darkroom",
 "case:locations.darkroom.summary": "Dr. Ellery’s darkroom, in the old scullery.",
 "case:locations.gardens.art.props.0.label": "House — south front",
 "case:locations.gardens.art.props.1.label": "Drawing room",
 "case:locations.gardens.art.props.2.label": "Scullery door",
 "case:locations.gardens.art.props.3.label": "Terrace",
 "case:locations.gardens.art.props.4.label": "Lawn",
 "case:locations.gardens.art.props.5.label": "Sundial",
 "case:locations.gardens.art.props.6.label": "Yew hedge",
 "case:locations.gardens.art.props.7.label": "Rose walk",
 "case:locations.gardens.art.props.8.label": "Glasshouse",
 "case:locations.gardens.art.props.9.label": "Walled garden",
 "case:locations.gardens.description": "The terrace runs the length of the south front. Below it the lawn falls forty yards to the glasshouse. To the east are the walled garden and the rose walk behind its tall yew hedge. The grass is still grey with dew.",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_garden_door.label": "Scullery door",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_garden_door.text": "A plain door at the east end of the house, onto the old scullery passage — the back stairs, the darkroom, the servants’ way. Anyone could come and go by it unseen from the drawing room, or the terrace.",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_rose.label": "Rose walk",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_rose.text": "A grass walk between old roses, behind a yew hedge ten feet high and thick as a wall. From here you can’t see so much as the roof of the glasshouse, though you would hear its bell well enough. A pleasant place to walk on a warm night, and nobody would see you there.",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_stokehole.label": "Stokehole door",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_stokehole.text": "Below the east end of the glasshouse, a short flight of brick steps drops to the stokehole door. Stout oak, with no keyhole and no handle on this side. Once it’s bolted from inside, you can’t get in. Once it’s unbolted, anyone can come out.",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_tripod.label": "Tripod marks",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_tripod.text": "Three small dents in the gravel at the edge of the terrace, where Dr. Ellery’s tripod stood. This is where the four of them watched from when the bell rang.",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_view.label": "View down the lawn",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_view.text": "From the terrace the glasshouse is forty yards off, down the slope of the lawn. Even this morning the glass is misted from the warmth inside. Last night, with the lamp lit, it must have glowed like a lantern.",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_wall_door.label": "Walled-garden door",
 "case:locations.gardens.hotspots.hs_gd_wall_door.text": "The door in the wall of the kitchen garden, beside the east end of the glasshouse. On the step, something has been knocked out and trodden on.",
 "case:locations.gardens.name": "The Terrace & Gardens",
 "case:locations.gardens.summary": "Where the household watched for the bell.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.0.label": "Glass wall",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.1.label": "Glass wall",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.10.label": "Stokehole steps",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.11.label": "Rainwater tank",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.12.label": "Door to the lawn",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.2.label": "Glass wall",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.3.label": "Heating pipes",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.4.label": "Staging",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.5.label": "Tree fern",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.6.label": "Staging",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.7.label": "Lamp bracket",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.8.label": "Orchid bench",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.art.props.9.label": "Log table",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.description": "Sir Edmund’s great glasshouse at the bottom of the lawn, built in 1912 for the plants he sent home. The boiler keeps it as warm as a jungle night. Sergeant Polglase has had the body taken to St Austell. The air still smells of clove and lily.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_ashpan.label": "Boiler ash pan",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_ashpan.text": "Somebody burned something in Jory’s boiler. You draw out the ash pan beneath the firebars and sift it with a trowel.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_bell.label": "Bell pull",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_bell.text": "By the door, a bell pull on a brass chain runs up to the ship’s bell on the roof ridge. One pull and the whole garden hears it. Anyone standing inside could ring it.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_door.label": "Glass door",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_door.text": "The door to the lawn locks from the inside. At dawn the key was in the lock, on the inside, and the door was locked. Jory turned it himself to run for help. Whoever left here last night didn’t leave this way.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_flask.label": "Cocoa flask",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_flask.text": "Agnes Quill’s flask of cocoa, one cup poured and drunk. The rest has gone cold, with a skin on it. Nothing in it but cocoa and too much sugar, the sergeant says. He has tasted it.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_ladder.label": "Stepladder",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_ladder.text": "A wooden stepladder lies on its side across the tiles, where the sergeant found it.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_lamp.label": "Lamp",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_lamp.text": "The paraffin lamp hangs from its iron bracket above the orchid bench, turned down to a blue bead. Jory filled it yesterday afternoon; there’s oil enough left in it for another night.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_log.label": "Log table",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_log.text": "A small bamboo table at the end of the path, with a ledger, a fountain pen, a sable brush and a bottle of green ink.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_orchid.label": "The moon orchid",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_orchid.text": "In pride of place on the bench, in a slatted teak basket: the moon orchid. One flower, and it is over.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_outline.label": "Chalk outline",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_outline.text": "Sergeant Polglase’s chalk outline at the foot of the orchid bench. She lay on her side with her veil over her hair and her shawl about her shoulders, Jory says, neat as if she’d sat down on the tiles to rest.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_prints.label": "Coke dust on the steps",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_prints.text": "Someone dressed as Lady Penhallow left this glasshouse locked behind them. You go back to the stokehole steps with a torch and look again, more carefully.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_staging.label": "Edge of the staging",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_staging.text": "The iron edge of the orchid bench, at the height of an old woman’s head — if she fell from the third step.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_stick.label": "Ebony stick",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_stick.text": "Under the bench, among the moss and the dropped labels, lies a walking stick with a silver knob.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_stokehole.label": "Stokehole steps",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.hotspots.hs_gh_stokehole.text": "At the east end, iron steps go down into the dark. The stokehole: the boiler, the coke heap, and a stout door that opens out at the foot of the walled-garden wall.",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.name": "The Glasshouse",
 "case:locations.glasshouse.summary": "Where Lady Penhallow kept her vigil, and died.",
 "case:locations.library.art.props.0.label": "Bookcases",
 "case:locations.library.art.props.1.label": "Sir Edmund",
 "case:locations.library.art.props.2.label": "Fireplace",
 "case:locations.library.art.props.3.label": "Armchair",
 "case:locations.library.art.props.4.label": "Armchair",
 "case:locations.library.art.props.5.label": "Desk",
 "case:locations.library.art.props.6.label": "Rug",
 "case:locations.library.art.props.7.label": "Drinks table",
 "case:locations.library.art.props.8.label": "Window",
 "case:locations.library.description": "Sir Edmund’s library: plant-hunting journals, botanical plates and forty years of Kew Bulletins. Nobody has tidied since last night. Two glasses still stand on the drinks table, and the room smells of cigar smoke.",
 "case:locations.library.hotspots.hs_lb_briefcase.label": "Mr. Kittow’s briefcase",
 "case:locations.library.hotspots.hs_lb_briefcase.text": "A pigskin briefcase stamped ‘E.K.’ stands by the desk, unlocked. Mr. Kittow told you to help yourself.",
 "case:locations.library.hotspots.hs_lb_glasses.label": "Two glasses",
 "case:locations.library.hotspots.hs_lb_glasses.text": "Two whisky glasses and Mrs. Pascoe’s tray. An ashtray full to the brim: cigar ends, and the stubs of a great many cigarettes. Kittow smokes cigars; Julian chain-smokes. They were here a long while.",
 "case:locations.library.hotspots.hs_lb_portrait.label": "Portrait of Sir Edmund",
 "case:locations.library.hotspots.hs_lb_portrait.text": "Sir Edmund Penhallow in 1938, painted in his bush jacket with a vasculum on his shoulder, laughing at something just out of the frame. Beneath it, in green ink on a card: ‘Missing, Shan Hills, March 1943. He always came home.’",
 "case:locations.library.hotspots.hs_lb_window.label": "Window",
 "case:locations.library.hotspots.hs_lb_window.text": "The library looks east over the stable yard. From here you can’t see the glasshouse, the terrace or the lawn. Whoever sat in this room last night saw nothing of the garden until they went out onto the terrace.",
 "case:locations.library.name": "The Library",
 "case:locations.library.summary": "Where Julian and Mr. Kittow argued over the will.",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.art.props.0.label": "Window over the lawn",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.art.props.1.label": "Writing desk",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.art.props.2.label": "Chair",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.art.props.3.label": "Herbarium cabinet",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.art.props.4.label": "Fireplace",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.art.props.5.label": "Rug",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.art.props.6.label": "Sofa",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.description": "A small, light room on the south front, where Hester did her letters and her botany. Pressed specimens are framed on every wall. From the window she could watch her glasshouse at the bottom of the lawn.",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_blotter.label": "Blotter",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_blotter.text": "A green leather blotter. Folded inside the cover, where she could find it and nobody else would, is a letter.",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_cabinet.label": "Herbarium cabinet",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_cabinet.text": "Edmund’s pressed specimens in mahogany drawers, each one labelled twice, in his brown ink and in her green. The bottom drawer holds a single yellowed telegram: ‘REGRET TO INFORM YOU SIR EDMUND PENHALLOW MISSING BELIEVED KILLED.’",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_desk.label": "Writing desk",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_desk.text": "Her letters, answered and unanswered, in two neat piles. One lies apart from the rest, under a paperweight shaped like a snail.",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_diary.label": "Engagement diary",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_diary.text": "Her engagement diary lies open at this week, in green: ‘Fri 26 — 6 pm, Celia. THE BLOOM!’ and ‘Sat 27 — 10 am, Edwin. The will.’ Under Friday, in a smaller hand: ‘Forgive him if he comes.’",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_ink.label": "Inkstand",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_ink.text": "A silver inkstand with two wells. Both are filled with green ink. There isn’t a bottle of any other colour in the room, nor a black pen.",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_window.label": "Window",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.hotspots.hs_sr_window.text": "From here you look straight down the lawn to the glasshouse. A south window, like Miss Quill’s next door. From this side of the house the bell, the lamp and the white flower would all have been in plain view.",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.lockedHint": "Locked. Agnes Quill has the key.",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.name": "Lady Penhallow’s Sitting Room",
 "case:locations.sitting_room.summary": "Her private room over the terrace.",
 "case:setting": "Penhallow House · Cornwall · July 1963",
 "case:sketchCredit": "SKETCH: SGT. W. POLGLASE · NOT TO SCALE",
 "case:sketchTitle": "PENHALLOW HO.",
 "case:stayLabel": "Stay at Penhallow",
 "case:tagline": "A botanist, a will, and an orchid that only blooms at night.",
 "case:timeline.conclusion": "Lady Penhallow was alive at 10.25 and dead before her orchid opened at about 11.05. Only one person is known to have been with her then: Celia, who went down at twenty to eleven and left by the stokehole a few minutes after the bell.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_bell.text": "The bell rings and the ‘widow’ raises her lamp to the terrace",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_bell.time": "11.32 pm",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_bloom.teaser": "What became of the flower.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_bloom.text": "The moon orchid opens — and no one pollinates it",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_celia_goes.teaser": "Someone went to the glasshouse between half past ten and eleven — and has kept quiet about it.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_celia_goes.text": "Celia slips down to the glasshouse",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_cocoa.teaser": "Someone saw her alive after she locked herself in.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_cocoa.text": "Agnes brings cocoa — Lady Penhallow is alive and well",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_cocoa.time": "10.15 pm",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_drawing_room.text": "Celia comes down to the drawing room in her dressing gown",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_drawing_room.time": "11.45 pm",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_found.teaser": "What the gardener did at dawn.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_found.text": "Jory finds the body — and pollinates the orchid",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_found.time": "5.45 am",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_julian.teaser": "Someone went down to the glasshouse after midnight.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_julian.text": "Julian finds the glasshouse locked and the lamp turned low",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_julian.time": "12.15 am",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_last_entry.teaser": "The last thing she is known to have done.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_last_entry.text": "Lady Penhallow writes her last entry in the bloom log",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_last_entry.time": "10.25 pm",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_lock.text": "Lady Penhallow locks herself in with her orchid",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_lock.time": "9.30 pm",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_stokehole.teaser": "Someone in the garden saw more than they’ve told.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_stokehole.text": "A pale figure slips out of the stokehole",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_struck.teaser": "How she really died.",
 "case:timeline.events.tl_struck.text": "Lady Penhallow is struck down with her own stick",
 "case:timeline.prompt": "Put Friday night’s events in the order they really happened. Times you know for certain are shown; reason out the rest.",
 "case:timeline.title": "The night of the bloom, reconstructed",
 "case:title": "The Glasshouse Widow",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_alive.insight": "Hester logged her orchid every quarter of an hour and wrote nothing after 10.25. Whoever left the stokehole went a few minutes after the bell, not at ten to eleven. Celia didn’t leave a living woman behind her.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_alive.response.0": "Then she… she must have stopped writing. She was tired. Old women do get tired.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_alive.response.1": "Her hands have found each other in her lap and are holding on tight. Hester Penhallow, by every account, had waited twenty-five years to write the next line.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_alive.title": "Hester was dead before Celia left",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_bed.insight": "The Blue Room faces north over the drive. Celia never watched Hester from her window. She wasn’t in bed when the bell rang.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_bed.response.0": "…I may have been confused. I had a sick headache.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_bed.response.1": "Very well. I wasn’t in bed. I couldn’t lie still, so I put on my dressing gown and slipped out into the garden — the rose walk, for the air. I was there when I heard the bell ring, and then I came in.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_bed.response.2": "I didn’t say so because Julian would have fussed. That’s all.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_bed.title": "Celia couldn’t see the glasshouse",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_knew.insight": "Hester told Celia about Julian’s forgery at six o’clock on Friday. In the morning the proof was to go to Mr. Kittow, and from him to the police.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_knew.response.0": "…She wrote it down? Of course she did. She wrote everything down.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_knew.response.1": "Yes. She sent for me at six and showed me the letter. She said Julian had the night to come to her and confess, and in the morning it would all go to Mr. Kittow. And then the police.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_knew.response.2": "I didn’t tell Julian. I thought I could talk her round myself. I always could talk people round. Everyone except Hester.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_knew.title": "Celia knew about the forgery",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_rose_walk.insight": "Celia wasn’t walking among the roses. She was in the glasshouse that night and left by the stokehole. She now admits she went to Hester at twenty to eleven, and swears she left her alive.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_rose_walk.response.0": "…",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_rose_walk.response.1": "All right. Yes. At twenty to eleven I went down to the glasshouse, and she let me in. I begged her not to sign that will — to think what it would do to Julian. She wouldn’t listen. She never listened to me.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_rose_walk.response.2": "I left at ten to eleven, by the stokehole, so that no one would see me crossing the lawn. She was alive. She was sitting there writing in that log of hers, as calm as you please.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_celia_rose_walk.title": "Celia was inside the glasshouse",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jory_bed.insight": "Jory wasn’t in bed. He stood at the walled-garden door half the night, watching the glass, and saw someone leave by the stokehole after the bell.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jory_bed.response.0": "…That’s mine. Can’t deny it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jory_bed.response.1": "I couldn’t sleep, not with her flower coming out. I stood at the walled-garden door from before eleven, smoking and watching the glass. I heard the bell, I saw the lamp go up and come down. Her ladyship had said keep away, so I’ve said nothing.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jory_bed.response.2": "And a few minutes after, something came up out of the stokehole — long and pale, like a nightgown — and hurried away along the wall towards the house. I took it for her ladyship’s ghost, God forgive me. I’d be laughed out of the parish if I said so.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_jory_bed.title": "Jory was watching the glasshouse",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_drawing_room.insight": "Julian lied: at a quarter past twelve he slipped out to the glasshouse. He says the door was locked, the lamp low, and his aunt didn’t answer.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_drawing_room.response.0": "…Oh, for heaven’s sake. Yes. All right.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_drawing_room.response.1": "At a quarter past twelve I went out for some air, and my feet took me down the lawn. I wanted one more go at her before Kittow got his pen out. The door was locked and the lamp was turned right down. I knocked, I called her, and she didn’t answer.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_drawing_room.response.2": "I didn’t say so because I knew exactly how it would look. And it does look exactly like that, doesn’t it?",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_drawing_room.title": "Julian went down to the glasshouse",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_forgery.insight": "In May Julian forged his aunt’s signature to borrow £20,000 against Home Farm. That is the ‘something criminal’ in her letter.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_forgery.response.0": "…Where did you get that?",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_forgery.response.1": "Penhallow Motors has been dying for two years. I needed twenty thousand by May or the bank would have had the lot. Home Farm was coming to me one day anyway — I told myself it was a loan against my own inheritance. I practised her signature for a week.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_forgery.response.2": "Yes. I forged it. But I didn’t kill her for it. I didn’t even know she’d found out — she never said a word to me.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_forgery.title": "Julian forged the mortgage",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_money.insight": "Julian’s business is failing: the bank is threatening proceedings unless he pays by the end of the month. He swears he has kept it afloat honestly.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_money.response.0": "…That’s my private correspondence. Oh, what’s the use.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_money.response.1": "All right. Penhallow Motors is on its knees. Nobody in Plymouth wants a new Zephyr when they can’t pay for the old one, and the bank wants six thousand by the end of the month.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_money.response.2": "But I’ve kept it afloat honestly, near enough. I’ve never done anything criminal, if that’s what you’re thinking.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_julian_money.title": "Julian is close to ruin",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kittow_professional.insight": "Edwin Kittow and Hester Penhallow were secretly engaged. And she had told him more about the crime in her family than he let on.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kittow_professional.response.0": "…Ah. She kept it in her blotter. How like her.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kittow_professional.response.1": "Yes. I asked her to marry me in 1946, and in 1950, and again this spring. This spring she said yes. September, at St Mewan’s. We meant to tell the family once the will was signed.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kittow_professional.response.2": "There is something else you should know. On Friday afternoon she told me she had proof of a crime in the family, and would give it to me this morning. She said she would tell ‘his wife’ at six, so that he might have the decency to come and confess to her first. She didn’t say his name. She didn’t need to.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_kittow_professional.title": "Kittow was going to marry her",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_marian_kew.insight": "Marian Ellery is no longer at Kew. She came to Penhallow for Lindqvist Orchids of Miami, who want the moon orchid’s seed ‘at any cost’.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_marian_kew.response.0": "…You’ve been through my satchel. Well. I’d have done the same.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_marian_kew.response.1": "Kew dismissed me last October. I published some of Hester’s results under my own name, and she wrote to the Director. Then she forgave me, which was worse, and asked me here anyway: she said Edmund’s flower deserved a proper record. Lindqvist pays my rent now. He wanted me to secure the seed for him.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_marian_kew.response.2": "‘At any cost’ is how Americans write cables. It doesn’t mean murder. And understand this: if I’d killed her, that flower would have been pollinated by eleven o’clock. I could no more have let it close untouched than burned it.",
 "deductions:contradictions.c_marian_kew.title": "Dr. Ellery works for an American nursery",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bell_ringer.conclusion": "The figure at the bell stood a head taller than Hester, walked without the stick she couldn’t take a step without, and showed a pale hem under the black. Whoever rang the bell wore her veil and shawl, put them back on her body afterwards, and wanted the house to believe she was alive at 11.32. Four people watched from the terrace. Who wasn’t there?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bell_ringer.options.0": "Lady Penhallow herself",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bell_ringer.options.1": "Someone wearing her veil and shawl",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bell_ringer.options.2": "Jory Trevenna, on his rounds",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bell_ringer.options.3": "No one — a trick of the lamp on the misted glass",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bell_ringer.proofLabels.0": "The figure at the bell",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bell_ringer.proofLabels.1": "Why it can’t have been Hester",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bell_ringer.question": "Who did the household see in the glasshouse at 11.32?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_bell_ringer.title": "A false widow at the bell",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dead_before_bloom.conclusion": "At dawn the moon orchid was still open and untouched. Hester meant to pollinate it the moment it opened, and had waited twenty-five years to do it. She was dead before it opened.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dead_before_bloom.options.0": "Yes — she pollinated it, as she’d planned",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dead_before_bloom.options.1": "Yes — four people saw her beside it at 11.32",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dead_before_bloom.options.2": "No — she would never have left it unpollinated",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dead_before_bloom.proofLabels.0": "The flower at dawn",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dead_before_bloom.proofLabels.1": "What she meant to do the moment it opened",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dead_before_bloom.question": "Was Lady Penhallow alive when her orchid opened?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_dead_before_bloom.title": "Dead before the flower opened",
 "deductions:deductions.d_forgery.conclusion": "The Home Farm mortgage is signed ‘Hester Penhallow’ in black ink, though she wrote in nothing but green. She told Celia at six o’clock. In the morning the deed was going to Kittow and the police, and that night somebody burned it in the stokehole.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_forgery.options.0": "Only her new will, cutting Julian out",
 "deductions:deductions.d_forgery.options.1": "Proof that Julian forged her signature on a £20,000 mortgage",
 "deductions:deductions.d_forgery.options.2": "Proof that Dr. Ellery was selling the orchid to America",
 "deductions:deductions.d_forgery.options.3": "Notice of her engagement to Mr. Kittow",
 "deductions:deductions.d_forgery.proofLabels.0": "What she found out",
 "deductions:deductions.d_forgery.proofLabels.1": "Proof the signature was forged",
 "deductions:deductions.d_forgery.question": "What did Lady Penhallow mean to hand her solicitor on Saturday morning?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_forgery.title": "Julian forged her name",
 "deductions:deductions.d_julian_alibi.conclusion": "Mrs. Pascoe heard Julian and Kittow quarrelling in the library from a quarter past ten until ten past eleven. Hester died in between. By the time Julian knocked at the glasshouse at 12.15, she had been dead for more than an hour.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_julian_alibi.options.0": "Yes — he was at the glasshouse at 12.15",
 "deductions:deductions.d_julian_alibi.options.1": "Yes — he slipped out of the library",
 "deductions:deductions.d_julian_alibi.options.2": "No — he was in the library with Kittow while she died",
 "deductions:deductions.d_julian_alibi.proofLabels.0": "Where Julian was",
 "deductions:deductions.d_julian_alibi.proofLabels.1": "When she died",
 "deductions:deductions.d_julian_alibi.question": "Could Julian have killed his aunt?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_julian_alibi.title": "Julian didn’t do it",
 "deductions:deductions.d_struck.conclusion": "Nobody climbed the stepladder, and the staging is clean. The round wound is the size of the silver knob of her stick, which someone wiped and slid under the bench. The fall was staged.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_struck.options.0": "She fell from the stepladder and struck the iron staging",
 "deductions:deductions.d_struck.options.1": "Struck down with the silver knob of her own stick",
 "deductions:deductions.d_struck.options.2": "Her heart gave out in the excitement",
 "deductions:deductions.d_struck.options.3": "Poisoned by the cocoa Agnes brought her",
 "deductions:deductions.d_struck.proofLabels.0": "The weapon",
 "deductions:deductions.d_struck.proofLabels.1": "Why it wasn’t a fall",
 "deductions:deductions.d_struck.question": "How did Lady Penhallow die?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_struck.title": "Struck down with her own stick",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.conclusion": "Her last entry was at 10.25, at the first scent. Forty minutes later the flower opened, and she never pollinated it. She died between 10.25 and about 11.05, half an hour before anyone ‘saw’ her at the bell.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.options.0": "Between 10.25 and about 11.05 pm",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.options.1": "Between 11.32 pm and 1.00 am",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.options.2": "Between 1.00 and 5.45 am",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.options.3": "Before Agnes brought her cocoa at 10.15",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.proofLabels.0": "Her last sign of life",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.proofLabels.1": "Why she was dead before it opened",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.question": "When did Lady Penhallow really die?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_time_of_death.title": "Dead by five past eleven",
 "deductions:deductions.d_way_out.conclusion": "The glass door was locked from inside, with the key in the lock. The only other way out is through the stokehole, and its door can only be unbolted from within. Someone went down those steps after the bell, unbolted the outer door and slipped away into the dark.",
 "deductions:deductions.d_way_out.options.0": "Through the glass door, locking it behind them with a second key",
 "deductions:deductions.d_way_out.options.1": "Down through the stokehole and out by its outer door",
 "deductions:deductions.d_way_out.options.2": "They hid in the stokehole until Jory came at dawn",
 "deductions:deductions.d_way_out.options.3": "Up through a roof ventilator",
 "deductions:deductions.d_way_out.proofLabels.0": "The only other way out",
 "deductions:deductions.d_way_out.proofLabels.1": "Someone used it that night",
 "deductions:deductions.d_way_out.question": "How did the bell-ringer leave a glasshouse locked from the inside?",
 "deductions:deductions.d_way_out.title": "Out through the stokehole",
 "dialogues:statements.st_agnes_celia.text": "Mrs. Celia missed the bell. She came down at a quarter to twelve in her pale-blue dressing gown, saying it had woken her.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_agnes_cocoa.text": "At a quarter past ten I took her cocoa. She was well, and writing her log every quarter of an hour.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_agnes_ink.text": "Since 1943 she wrote everything in green ink — letters, labels, cheques. Never any other colour.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_agnes_lawn.text": "At a quarter past twelve I saw Mr. Julian from my window, walking down the lawn towards the glasshouse.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_agnes_stick.text": "Her ladyship couldn’t take a single step without her stick, and hadn’t been up a ladder in ten years.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_celia_bed.text": "From half past ten I was in bed. The bell woke me, and I watched Hester from my window.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_celia_didnt_know.text": "I know nothing whatever about any forgery or mortgage.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_celia_knew.text": "Hester told me at six o’clock that Julian had forged her name, and that in the morning the proof would go to Mr. Kittow.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_celia_plead.text": "At twenty to eleven I went to the glasshouse to plead with Hester over the will. When I left at ten to eleven she was alive, writing in her log.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_celia_rose_walk.text": "I couldn’t sleep, so I walked in the rose walk until I heard the bell, then came in. I never went near the glasshouse.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_jory_bed.text": "I banked the boiler at nine and was in my bed by ten.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_jory_dawn.text": "At a quarter to six the moon orchid was still open, and nobody had pollinated it. I did it myself, with her brush.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_jory_figure.text": "A few minutes after the lamp went down, I saw a pale figure in something long come up out of the stokehole and hurry away along the wall towards the house.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_jory_ladder.text": "Her ladyship never climbed those steps. They hang on their hook by the door, and hadn’t come down since Whitsun.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_jory_stokehole.text": "I bolted the stokehole door at nine. At dawn it was unbolted, and somebody had burned something in my boiler.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_julian_debts.text": "Penhallow Motors is in trouble, but I’ve kept it afloat honestly. I’ve never done anything criminal.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_julian_drawing_room.text": "After the bell I never left the drawing room until we all went up at one.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_julian_forgery.text": "I forged Aunt Hester’s signature on a £20,000 mortgage of Home Farm.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_julian_glasshouse.text": "At a quarter past twelve I went down to the glasshouse. The door was locked, the lamp turned low, and she didn’t answer.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_julian_library.text": "From a quarter past ten to a quarter past eleven I was arguing with Kittow in the library.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_julian_money.text": "My affairs are in perfectly good order. I didn’t need Aunt Hester’s money.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_julian_will.text": "Aunt Hester was signing a new will this morning that cut me out of Penhallow.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kittow_engaged.text": "Hester and I were to be married in September. She told me she would tell Celia about Julian’s crime at six on Friday, so he could confess before Saturday.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kittow_library.text": "From a quarter past ten until a quarter past eleven I was in the library with Julian.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kittow_professional.text": "My relations with Lady Penhallow were entirely professional.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_kittow_will.text": "Under the new will Julian would have had £1,000. Under the old one, which now stands, he inherits Penhallow.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_marian_darkroom.text": "From ten until ten past eleven I was in my darkroom. Then I set up my camera on the terrace.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_marian_kew.text": "I’m here on behalf of Kew, to record the bloom.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_marian_lindqvist.text": "Kew dismissed me last year. I’m paid by Lindqvist Orchids of Miami, who want the seed.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_marian_photo.text": "I took one photograph from the terrace the moment the bell rang. The print is drying in my darkroom.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_marian_pollinate.text": "The moon orchid opens about forty minutes after its first scent. Hester would have pollinated it the instant it opened — she’d sooner have died than miss it.",
 "dialogues:statements.st_marian_scent.text": "When Celia came into the drawing room at 11.45 she smelled strongly of the moon orchid, as if she’d been standing over it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_evening.lines.0": "At a quarter past ten I took her a flask of cocoa. She’d said no one was to come near, but she’d eaten nothing at dinner. She scolded me and let me in, and drank a cup while I stood there. She’d been writing up her log every quarter of an hour, happy as a girl. Then she locked the door behind me.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_evening.lines.1": "I was on the terrace with the others when the bell rang. I went up to bed at midnight, and at a quarter past twelve I looked out of my window and saw Mr. Julian on the lawn in the moonlight, walking down towards the glasshouse. I told the sergeant so. I’m sorry for it now.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_evening.prompt": "Tell me about last night.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_hester.lines.0": "Her hip was very bad. She couldn’t take one step without her stick, not even across her own bedroom — and she hadn’t been up a ladder in ten years. That’s what Jory was for.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_hester.lines.1": "And she wrote in green ink. Only green, ever since the telegram came in ’43 — it was Edmund’s colour. Her letters, her labels, her cheques. Green.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_hester.prompt": "What were Lady Penhallow’s habits?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_rooms.lines.0": "Her sitting room is locked. She kept the only spare key with me. I suppose it doesn’t matter now.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_rooms.lines.1": "She takes a small key from her pocket and holds it a moment before she gives it to you. Lady Penhallow’s Sitting Room is now open to you.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_rooms.prompt": "May I see Lady Penhallow’s rooms?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_terrace.lines.0": "Mr. Julian, Mr. Kittow, Dr. Ellery with her camera, and myself. We saw her lift the lamp and wave us away, and we went back in.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_terrace.lines.1": "Mrs. Celia had gone up at half past ten with a headache. She came down to the drawing room at a quarter to twelve in her pale-blue dressing gown, all eyes, and said the bell had woken her and she’d watched from her window.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_terrace.prompt": "Who was on the terrace when the bell rang?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_will.lines.0": "Under the old will, two thousand pounds. Under the new one, the lodge for my lifetime and three hundred a year. She told me so in June, and I cried like a child.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_will.lines.1": "So if you’re wondering, I had every reason in the world to want her alive until ten o’clock this morning.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_agnes_will.prompt": "Did Lady Penhallow provide for you?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_evening.lines.0": "Upstairs, in bed. I had the most appalling head — I said goodnight at half past ten and went straight up.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_evening.lines.1": "The bell woke me. I went to the window, and there she was — down in the glasshouse with her lamp, beside that enormous white flower. Like something out of Shakespeare. Then I couldn’t sleep, so I put on my dressing gown and came down to the others.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_evening.prompt": "Where were you last night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_forgery.lines.0": "Forged? Julian? Don’t be absurd. Julian couldn’t forge a sick note.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_forgery.lines.1": "I know nothing whatever about any mortgage. Julian keeps the business to himself. Ask him.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_forgery.prompt": "Did you know your husband forged his aunt’s name?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_hester.lines.0": "She thought Julian had married beneath him. An actress! She never said so. She didn’t need to.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_hester.lines.1": "But she could be kind. She showed me her orchid once, in May, and talked to it as if Edmund were standing there. I rather envied her, to tell you the truth. Fancy loving someone that much.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_hester.prompt": "How did you get on with Lady Penhallow?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_stage.lines.0": "Eleven years in rep. Bristol, Bath, a season at Stratford. I was a rather good Lady Macbeth, though I say it myself.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_stage.lines.1": "The sleepwalking scene. Every night, the whole house held its breath. Then I married Julian, and Penhallow Motors, and that was that.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_celia_stage.prompt": "You were on the stage, I hear.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_dawn.lines.0": "I come at a quarter to six to stoke up, same as every morning, by the stokehole. The outer door was unbolted — I’ll come to that. I went up the steps, and there was her ladyship, lying by the bench with the steps beside her. Cold.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_dawn.lines.1": "And the flower was open still, just. White as a plate. No one had touched it — her brush lying by the book, clean, the pollen still sitting in the flower. Twenty-five years she waited for that night.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_dawn.lines.2": "So I did it for her. With her own brush, the way she showed me, and tied it off with a bit of twine. It closed up not five minutes after. Then I ran for the house.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_dawn.prompt": "Tell me how you found her.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_evening.lines.0": "I banked the boiler at nine and went home. In my bed by ten, same as always. Her ladyship didn’t want nobody near the glass, and I do what I’m told.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_evening.prompt": "Where were you last night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_julian.lines.0": "Holiday camp. Chalets on Sir Edmund’s lawn and a swimming bath in my kitchen garden. That’s what he wants.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_julian.lines.1": "But I’ll say this for him: he’s no more stomach for blood than a hen. He fainted at a pig-killing when he was twelve.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_julian.prompt": "What do you make of Mr. Julian?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_ladder.lines.0": "Her ladyship? Not in ten years. Couldn’t, with that hip. Anything high, she’d call me.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_ladder.lines.1": "And there was nothing up there to climb to. The lamp hangs on the bracket where I put it. Those steps live on their hook by the door. Last time they came down was Whitsun.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_ladder.prompt": "Would she have climbed the stepladder?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_stokehole.lines.0": "I bolt that door at nine every night of my life. It’s only got a bolt on the inside, see — you can’t open it from the garden, nor shut it. This morning it was standing unbolted.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_stokehole.lines.1": "And somebody’d been at my boiler. Fire door open, and the fire roaring up like a furnace when I’d left it banked low. Somebody burned something in there.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_jory_stokehole.prompt": "What about the stokehole door?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_aunt.lines.0": "Twenty years in black for a man who died in a jungle. She talked to his plants. I’m not being cruel — she did. Out loud, every morning.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_aunt.lines.1": "She was clever, mind you. Cleverer than Uncle Edmund, Kittow says, and Kittow ought to know. She just never let anyone see it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_aunt.prompt": "What was your aunt like?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_celia.lines.0": "Celia? God, no. I’d have died first. She thinks the business is doing splendidly — I’ve told her so every week for a year.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_celia.lines.1": "She went up early last night with one of her heads. She’s been sleeping badly all summer. That’s my fault too, I suppose.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_celia.prompt": "Did your wife know about the mortgage?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_evening.lines.0": "Where were you after dinner, Mr. Penhallow?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_evening.lines.1": "In the library with Kittow from a quarter past ten, trying to make him see sense about that will. It got rather loud, I’m afraid. Then we all trooped out onto the terrace to wait for Aunt Hester’s bell.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_evening.lines.2": "When it rang we saw her, plain as day, holding up her lamp. Then we went back in and sat about drinking champagne until one, waiting for her to ring again. I never left the drawing room until the rest of us went up.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_evening.prompt": "Where were you last night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_glasshouse.lines.0": "The door was locked. The lamp inside had been turned right down to a glimmer; I could just make out the white of that flower. I knocked twice and called her name. Nothing.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_glasshouse.lines.1": "I thought she was sulking, or had dropped off in her chair. I didn’t go round the back — I didn’t know there was a back. I came away.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_glasshouse.lines.2": "The smell, though. That orchid of hers. It came through the glass like a wall.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_glasshouse.prompt": "Tell me exactly what you found at the glasshouse.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_money.lines.0": "Penhallow Motors? Splendidly. Best year we’ve had. Everyone wants a Zephyr.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_money.lines.1": "If you’re asking whether I needed Aunt Hester’s money, the answer’s no. My affairs are in perfectly good order.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_money.prompt": "How is your business doing?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_quarrel.lines.0": "She announced over the soup that she was signing a new will at ten this morning. Penhallow to the National Trust, lock, stock and glasshouse. Uncle Edmund promised me this place when I was nine years old.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_quarrel.lines.1": "I have a perfectly good offer for it from Sunnyside Holiday Camps. Chalets on the lawn, a swimming pool where the kitchen garden is. People need holidays. She looked at me as if I’d suggested selling Edmund’s bones.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_quarrel.lines.2": "So yes, we had words. I said she’d regret it. That isn’t a threat. It’s what people say.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_julian_quarrel.prompt": "What was the quarrel at dinner about?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_evening.lines.0": "In the library with Julian from a quarter past ten until a quarter past eleven, being shouted at. He wanted me to persuade Hester not to sign. I told him that a solicitor’s duty is to his client’s wishes, not her heir’s.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_evening.lines.1": "Mrs. Pascoe brought us whisky at twenty to eleven, and I dare say she heard every word. Then we went out to the terrace for the bell, and back to the drawing room until one.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_evening.prompt": "Where were you last night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_hester.lines.0": "I have acted for the family for forty years. I drew up Sir Edmund’s will before he sailed in 1941, and Hester’s after the telegram came.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_hester.lines.1": "My relations with Lady Penhallow were entirely professional, I assure you.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_hester.prompt": "How well did you know Lady Penhallow?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_hester_heart.lines.0": "She laughed more this summer than in the whole of the twenty years before it. She’d decided she was allowed to.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_hester_heart.lines.1": "She said the orchid was Edmund’s, and after last night she could let him rest. Then she would wear grey. Just grey, to begin with.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_hester_heart.prompt": "What was she like, Edwin — really?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_will.lines.0": "It was to be signed at ten this morning, before Mrs. Pascoe and the vicar. Never a witness who benefits — I’m left Edmund’s watch, so I couldn’t sign it myself. It leaves Penhallow and its gardens to the National Trust, with an endowment. Julian receives a thousand pounds and his uncle’s books.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_will.lines.1": "Under the old will — the will of 1950, which now stands — he inherits everything. You’ll find the draft in my briefcase in the library. I have no secrets from the police, or from you.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_kittow_will.prompt": "Tell me about the new will.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_bell.lines.0": "Hester, with the lamp held up beside the flower. She raised her hand to us — rather grandly, I thought, for her. Then she turned away and the lamp went down.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_bell.lines.1": "I took one exposure, the moment the bell went. Half a second at full aperture, through misted glass at forty yards, so don’t expect much. I developed it first thing this morning. The print’s on the line in my darkroom.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_bell.prompt": "What did you see when the bell rang?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_evening.lines.0": "I photographed Hester with the bud at twenty-five past nine, before she locked herself in. Then from ten until about ten past eleven I was in my darkroom developing it — Mrs. Pascoe came knocking at one point, poor woman, and I shouted at her.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_evening.lines.1": "After that I set up my tripod on the terrace to wait for the bell. It came later than I expected. I’d have sworn that flower would be open by eleven.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_evening.prompt": "Where were you last night?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_kew.lines.0": "That’s right. The Herbarium wants a full record of the flower — photographs, measurements, the timing. Hester and I have corresponded about the plant for five years.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_kew.lines.1": "Under the new will the orchid was going to Kew. That’s why I’m here.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_kew.prompt": "You’re here on behalf of Kew?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_lindqvist.lines.0": "The seed. A pod from that flower holds a million seeds, and every seedling would sell in Florida for a hundred dollars. Lindqvist wanted to own the moon orchid, the way a man owns a racehorse.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_lindqvist.lines.1": "I was going to ask Hester to sell him a share of the seed. Legally, and honestly. She’d have refused, of course. She’d already left it all to Kew.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_lindqvist.prompt": "What exactly did Lindqvist want?",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_orchid.lines.0": "Edmund Penhallow collected it in the Shan Hills in 1938. The valley it came from was burnt out in the war. As far as anyone knows, Hester’s plant is the last one alive. It had never once flowered in England.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_orchid.lines.1": "It opens a single time, after dark. From the first scent to the open flower takes about forty minutes, and it closes for good when the sun comes up. The only chance to pollinate it is while it’s open — and the moment it opens is the best chance of all.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_orchid.lines.2": "Hester had waited twenty-five years for last night. She’d have pollinated it the instant it opened. She’d sooner have died than miss it.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_orchid.prompt": "Tell me about the moon orchid.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_scent.lines.0": "Now you ask… yes. When she came into the drawing room she brought the scent of the orchid in with her. Clove and lily — I’d know it anywhere. Strong, as if she’d been standing over the flower.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_scent.lines.1": "I thought it must have drifted up through the windows. But the drawing-room windows were shut by then, and the glasshouse is forty yards away. That scent clings to whatever has been near it. It doesn’t travel.",
 "dialogues:topics.t_marian_scent.prompt": "When Celia came down at 11.45, did you notice anything?",
 "evidence:evidence.bank_demand.detail": "Crumpled in the inside pocket of Julian’s dinner jacket: a letter from the Westminster Bank, Plymouth, dated 22nd July 1963.\n\n‘Dear Mr. Penhallow — We must again draw your attention to the overdrawn account of Penhallow Motors, which now stands at £6,412 7s. 2d. Unless it is cleared by 31st July, we shall have no alternative but to commence legal proceedings…’\n\nIt has been folded and unfolded many times.",
 "evidence:evidence.bank_demand.name": "Letter from the Westminster Bank",
 "evidence:evidence.bank_demand.summary": "A final demand to Penhallow Motors, threatening ‘legal proceedings’.",
 "evidence:evidence.bloom_log.detail": "Hester’s log of the bloom, in green ink, in a small upright hand:\n\n‘9.30 — Locked in. Lamp lit. Bud swelling.\n9.45 — Tip of the bud colouring.\n10.00 — Edmund’s hour. Bud loosening.\n10.15 — Agnes with cocoa, against orders. Bless her.\n10.25 — Sepals parting. First scent: clove and lily. Forty minutes, Edmund always said. I shall pollinate the moment it opens.’\n\nAfter 10.25 the page is blank. Her pen lies beside the log, capped. Her sable pollinating brush lies beside it.",
 "evidence:evidence.bloom_log.name": "The bloom log",
 "evidence:evidence.bloom_log.summary": "Green ink every quarter of an hour — until 10.25, then nothing.",
 "evidence:evidence.blue_room_window.detail": "The Blue Room, where Julian and Celia sleep, is on the north front. Its only window looks over the gravel drive and the beech avenue.\n\nThe glasshouse lies to the south, at the bottom of the lawn, on the far side of the house. From this room you can’t see so much as its weathervane.",
 "evidence:evidence.blue_room_window.name": "The Blue Room window",
 "evidence:evidence.blue_room_window.summary": "Celia’s window faces north, over the drive. The glasshouse is on the other side of the house.",
 "evidence:evidence.celia_mules.detail": "At the back of the wardrobe, pushed behind the evening dresses: a pair of pale-blue satin mules and the matching dressing gown.\n\nThe soles of the mules are grey with coke dust, ground into the satin. The hem of the dressing gown is smudged black all round. Someone has tried to brush it, and only spread it.",
 "evidence:evidence.celia_mules.name": "Celia’s dressing gown and mules",
 "evidence:evidence.celia_mules.summary": "Pale-blue satin — and black coke dust on the soles and the hem.",
 "evidence:evidence.deed_fragment.detail": "A scorched corner of heavy legal paper that fell through the firebars into the ash pan:\n\n‘…MORTGAGE of the property known as Home Farm, Penhallow… the sum of Twenty Thousand Pounds… Lorimer & Dane… signed by the said Hester Penhallow…’\n\nThe signature survives at the edge, burnt brown: ‘Hester Penhallow’, in black ink.",
 "evidence:evidence.deed_fragment.name": "Charred deed",
 "evidence:evidence.deed_fragment.summary": "From the boiler’s ash pan: ‘…Home Farm… £20,000… Hester Penhallow’ — signed in black ink.",
 "evidence:evidence.ebony_stick.detail": "Her ebony walking stick, found under the orchid bench. Its knob is solid silver, round, about the size of a half-crown, and engraved all over with a pattern of leaves.\n\nThe knob has been wiped. But down in the engraving there is a dark smear of dried blood, and two white hairs are caught in it.",
 "evidence:evidence.ebony_stick.name": "Lady Penhallow’s stick",
 "evidence:evidence.ebony_stick.summary": "A heavy silver knob, the size of a half-crown — with blood in the chasing.",
 "evidence:evidence.finance_letter.detail": "Lorimer & Dane, Finance, London E.C.2. 19th July 1963.\n\n‘Dear Lady Penhallow — We enclose for your records the counterpart deed of your mortgage of Home Farm, executed on 2nd May, under which the sum of £20,000 was advanced to your nephew, Mr. Julian Penhallow, as your agent…’\n\nAcross the foot, in green ink:\n\n‘I never signed this. Julian, of course. I shall tell Celia on Friday at six, and give him the night to come to me himself. On Saturday it goes to Edwin, and from Edwin to the police.’\n\nThe counterpart deed itself isn’t with the letter.",
 "evidence:evidence.finance_letter.name": "Letter from Lorimer & Dane",
 "evidence:evidence.finance_letter.summary": "About ‘her’ £20,000 mortgage of Home Farm, and her note in green: ‘I never signed this.’",
 "evidence:evidence.hester_letter.detail": "Penhallow House, St Austell. 23rd July 1963.\n\n‘Dear Sir or Madam — I am told you are discreet. I have discovered that a member of my family has done something criminal. On Saturday morning I mean to put the proof into my solicitor’s hands, together with a new will. Until then I should feel easier with someone in the house who is not family.\n\nThe moon orchid my husband sent home in 1938 will flower on Friday night, for the first time in this country. Come on Friday, and you shall see it.\n\nYours faithfully, Hester Penhallow’\n\nWritten in green ink. It reached your office on Friday afternoon.",
 "evidence:evidence.hester_letter.name": "Lady Penhallow’s letter",
 "evidence:evidence.hester_letter.summary": "Posted on Tuesday: ‘a member of my family has done something criminal’.",
 "evidence:evidence.iron_staging.detail": "The cast-iron edge of the staging, where Dr. Tresidder says she struck her head as she fell. You go over it with a lens.\n\nNo blood, no hair, not a scrape in the old green paint. And its edge is a sharp right angle — nothing like a round wound the size of a half-crown.",
 "evidence:evidence.iron_staging.name": "The iron staging",
 "evidence:evidence.iron_staging.summary": "Where she supposedly struck her head: not a mark on it.",
 "evidence:evidence.jory_pipe.detail": "By the walled-garden door, on the step: a little heap of pipe dottle and half a dozen spent matches. The tobacco is Cornish twist, black and strong. Jory Trevenna smokes nothing else.\n\nFrom this step you can see the whole east end of the glasshouse — and the stokehole door below it.",
 "evidence:evidence.jory_pipe.name": "Pipe dottle by the walled-garden door",
 "evidence:evidence.jory_pipe.summary": "Fresh pipe ash and spent matches where the walled garden looks onto the glasshouse.",
 "evidence:evidence.kittow_letter.detail": "Folded inside her blotter, in a lawyer’s careful hand:\n\n‘My dear Hester — Saturday, then, once the will is signed and your orchid is safe, let us tell them. Forty years I have waited; I can wait until the flower has had its night. September at St Mewan’s, as you wished. — Your Edwin’",
 "evidence:evidence.kittow_letter.name": "Letter from Edwin Kittow",
 "evidence:evidence.kittow_letter.summary": "‘…once the will is signed and your orchid is safe, let us tell them.’",
 "evidence:evidence.lindqvist_cable.detail": "A cable form, folded small in Dr. Ellery’s satchel, received at St Austell on Thursday:\n\n‘ELLERY PENHALLOW HOUSE ST AUSTELL — OFFER STANDS TWENTY THOUSAND DOLLARS EXCLUSIVE FIRST SEEDLINGS STOP SECURE POD AT ANY COST STOP — LINDQVIST ORCHIDS MIAMI’",
 "evidence:evidence.lindqvist_cable.name": "Cable from Miami",
 "evidence:evidence.lindqvist_cable.summary": "To Dr. Ellery: ‘Twenty thousand dollars … secure pod at any cost.’",
 "evidence:evidence.moon_orchid.detail": "Edmund’s orchid, label in his hand: ‘No. 7 — Shan Hills, 1938’. The great white flower has closed and hangs limp. The air around it still smells faintly of clove and lily.\n\nA twist of green garden twine has been freshly tied around the stem — a gardener’s sign that a flower has been pollinated. Lady Penhallow’s own twine is silk, and white.",
 "evidence:evidence.moon_orchid.name": "The moon orchid",
 "evidence:evidence.moon_orchid.summary": "Closed and hanging now. A twist of green twine marks it as pollinated.",
 "evidence:evidence.pascoe_statement.detail": "Statement of Mrs. Morwenna Pascoe, cook-housekeeper, taken by Sgt. Polglase:\n\n‘From a quarter past ten I was in the butler’s pantry next to the library, cleaning the silver for the morning. Mr. Julian and Mr. Kittow were going at it hammer and tongs over the will the whole time — I could hear every word through the wall. I took them in a tray of whisky at twenty to eleven, and they were still at it at ten past eleven when I went down to my kitchen.\n\nAt ten to eleven I stepped along to the old scullery that Dr. Ellery has made her darkroom, to ask did she want cocoa. She called through the door she had paper in the developer and I wasn’t to open it on any account.\n\nAfter that I was in my kitchen until I heard the bell.’",
 "evidence:evidence.pascoe_statement.name": "Statement of Mrs. Pascoe",
 "evidence:evidence.pascoe_statement.summary": "The cook-housekeeper: the library row from 10.15 to 11.10, and Dr. Ellery in her darkroom at 10.50.",
 "evidence:evidence.photo_bell.detail": "Dr. Ellery’s single long exposure from the terrace when the bell rang, through misted glass at forty yards. Grainy, but the shapes are plain: the orchid bench, the iron lamp bracket above it, and the white star of the open flower.\n\nBeside it stands a figure in a black veil and shawl, lamp held high in one hand, the other hand raised. It stands upright, so tall that its head is level with the lamp bracket. There is no stick. And below the black shawl a hem shows pale against the dark.",
 "evidence:evidence.photo_bell.name": "Photograph, 11.32 pm",
 "evidence:evidence.photo_bell.summary": "The ‘widow’ at the bell: tall, upright, no stick — and a pale hem under the black.",
 "evidence:evidence.photo_bud.detail": "Dr. Ellery’s photograph taken inside the glasshouse at 9.25, just before Hester locked herself in.\n\nA small, stooped woman in black leans on her ebony stick beside the orchid bench. The top of her veil barely reaches the shelf below the iron lamp bracket. She is black from veil to hem.",
 "evidence:evidence.photo_bud.name": "Photograph, 9.25 pm",
 "evidence:evidence.photo_bud.summary": "Lady Penhallow with her orchid in bud: small, bent over her stick, black from veil to hem.",
 "evidence:evidence.sergeant_notes.detail": "Friday 26 July. Fine, warm, no wind.\n\n8.00 pm — Dinner. Deceased, Mr. J. Penhallow (nephew), Mrs. C. Penhallow, Dr. M. Ellery, Mr. E. Kittow (solicitor), Miss A. Quill (companion). Words between deceased and nephew over a new will.\n9.30 pm — Deceased locks herself in glasshouse to watch her orchid flower. Says she will ring the bell once when it opens, and again when the household may come down to see it.\n10.15 pm — Miss Quill takes her cocoa.\n10.30 pm — Mrs. C. Penhallow retires with a headache.\n11.32 pm — Glasshouse bell. Deceased seen from terrace beside her flower, lamp in hand, by Mr. J. Penhallow, Mr. Kittow, Miss Quill and Dr. Ellery. Lamp lowered c. 11.35.\n11.45 pm — Mrs. C. Penhallow comes down to drawing room.\n12.15 am — Nephew seen on lawn going towards glasshouse (Miss Quill, from her window).\n5.45 am — J. Trevenna, gardener, finds deceased at foot of stepladder below orchid bench. Glasshouse door locked inside, key in lock. Stokehole door unbolted.\n\nDr. Tresidder: fell from the steps and struck her head on the iron staging. Wound above left ear, round, size of a half-crown. Death between 11.30 pm and 2 am.\n\nNephew had motive, was out alone after midnight, and the stokehole was open. — Sgt. W. Polglase",
 "evidence:evidence.sergeant_notes.name": "Sergeant Polglase’s notes",
 "evidence:evidence.sergeant_notes.summary": "The night as the police have it — and their suspect.",
 "evidence:evidence.slipper_prints.detail": "The stokehole steps are thick with coke dust. Jory’s hobnailed boots have trampled most of it. But at the edges of the treads there are prints of quite another kind: small, smooth and heelless, like a lady’s mule or slipper, going down.\n\nNobody goes down to a boiler in their slippers.",
 "evidence:evidence.slipper_prints.name": "Prints in the coke dust",
 "evidence:evidence.slipper_prints.summary": "A small, heelless slipper went down the stokehole steps.",
 "evidence:evidence.stepladder.detail": "A wooden stepladder lying on its side beside the chalk outline. It usually hangs on a hook by the door.\n\nIts treads are grey with a summer’s dust, and the dust is perfect: not one footprint, not one smudge. Nobody climbed this ladder last night.",
 "evidence:evidence.stepladder.name": "The stepladder",
 "evidence:evidence.stepladder.summary": "Lying beside the body — but no one has climbed it.",
 "evidence:evidence.stokehole_door.detail": "Steps at the east end of the glasshouse lead down to the stokehole, where the boiler is. From there a stout oak door opens out at the foot of the walled-garden wall.\n\nIts only bolt is on the inside. It can’t be fastened, or opened, from the garden. At dawn it was found unbolted.",
 "evidence:evidence.stokehole_door.name": "The stokehole door",
 "evidence:evidence.stokehole_door.summary": "The way out under the glasshouse: bolted on the inside only — and found unbolted.",
 "evidence:evidence.will_draft.detail": "From Mr. Kittow’s briefcase, ready for signature ‘at 10 a.m., Saturday 27th July 1963’, revoking the will of 1950 (under which Penhallow passes to Julian):\n\n— Penhallow House, its gardens and glasshouses, to the National Trust, with £40,000 for their upkeep.\n— To Julian Penhallow: £1,000, and Sir Edmund’s books.\n— To Agnes Quill: the lodge for her lifetime, and £300 a year.\n— To Jory Trevenna: his cottage, and £500.\n— To Edwin Kittow: Sir Edmund’s gold watch.\n— The moon orchid, and all its seed, to Kew.",
 "evidence:evidence.will_draft.name": "Draft of the new will",
 "evidence:evidence.will_draft.summary": "To be signed at ten this morning: Penhallow to the National Trust; Julian, £1,000.",
 "suspects:suspects.agnes.bio": "Lady Penhallow’s companion for twenty years, since the telegram from Burma. She runs the household, reads aloud in the evenings and knows every one of Hester’s habits. She took her a flask of cocoa last night, against orders.",
 "suspects:suspects.agnes.dismissal": "I’m sorry. I don’t see what you mean.",
 "suspects:suspects.agnes.name": "Agnes Quill",
 "suspects:suspects.agnes.rebuttal": "I was standing on the terrace with the others when the bell rang. I’ve looked after her for twenty years. Under the new will she was giving me the lodge — I had every reason to want her to sign it.",
 "suspects:suspects.agnes.role": "The companion",
 "suspects:suspects.celia.bio": "Julian’s wife of eight years. Before her marriage she was an actress in repertory — Bristol, Bath, a season at Stratford. Graceful, quick and watchful. She went up early last night with a headache.",
 "suspects:suspects.celia.dismissal": "Darling, you’ll have to do better than that.",
 "suspects:suspects.celia.name": "Celia Penhallow",
 "suspects:suspects.celia.rebuttal": "Me? Four people saw Hester alive at half past eleven, and I was with them the rest of the night. You’ve been reading too many detective stories.",
 "suspects:suspects.celia.role": "The nephew’s wife",
 "suspects:suspects.jory.bio": "He came to Penhallow as a garden boy of fourteen, the year Sir Edmund built the glasshouse, and has been head gardener for thirty years. He stokes its boiler at dusk and at dawn. He found her ladyship at a quarter to six this morning.",
 "suspects:suspects.jory.dismissal": "That don’t prove nothing, begging your pardon.",
 "suspects:suspects.jory.name": "Jory Trevenna",
 "suspects:suspects.jory.rebuttal": "Me? I’d have died for her, and for that orchid. Whoever came out of that stokehole was in a sight more of a hurry than an old man with a bad knee.",
 "suspects:suspects.jory.role": "The head gardener",
 "suspects:suspects.julian.bio": "Sir Edmund’s nephew and, under the present will, heir to Penhallow. He sells motor cars in Plymouth, and has a buyer for the estate lined up: a holiday-camp company. He quarrelled with his aunt at dinner.",
 "suspects:suspects.julian.dismissal": "Is that supposed to mean something? I sell cars, old thing. I know a dud when I see one.",
 "suspects:suspects.julian.name": "Julian Penhallow",
 "suspects:suspects.julian.rebuttal": "I was in the library with Kittow from a quarter past ten — ask him, ask Mrs. Pascoe. I’ve done things I’m ashamed of. I never laid a finger on Aunt Hester.",
 "suspects:suspects.julian.role": "The nephew",
 "suspects:suspects.kittow.bio": "Senior partner of Kittow & Pengelly, Truro, and the Penhallows’ solicitor for forty years. A widower, courteous and unhurried. He came to stay so that the new will could be signed at ten this morning.",
 "suspects:suspects.kittow.dismissal": "I have spent forty years listening to arguments. That one would not detain a magistrate.",
 "suspects:suspects.kittow.name": "Edwin Kittow",
 "suspects:suspects.kittow.rebuttal": "I was in the library arguing with Julian when you say she died, and Mrs. Pascoe saw us both. I would have married her in September. Look elsewhere — and look carefully.",
 "suspects:suspects.kittow.role": "The solicitor",
 "suspects:suspects.marian.bio": "An orchid specialist, here — she says — on behalf of the Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew, to photograph and record the bloom. Lady Penhallow’s correspondent and protégée for five years.",
 "suspects:suspects.marian.dismissal": "That’s not evidence. That’s an anecdote.",
 "suspects:suspects.marian.name": "Dr. Marian Ellery",
 "suspects:suspects.marian.rebuttal": "I was on the terrace taking the photograph when she rang that bell — you’ve seen it. And I would never, ever have let that flower close untouched. Whoever did this didn’t care about it at all.",
 "suspects:suspects.marian.role": "The botanist"
}
